# Line endings are decided here rather than by whatever `core.autocrlf` a contributor happens to
# have. `text=auto eol=lf` stores every text file with LF *and* checks it out with LF, on every
# platform. The second half is what makes the rule enforceable: with the working tree normalised,
# `.prettierrc` can set `endOfLine: "lf"` and `npm run format:check` fails on a CRLF file instead
# of accepting whatever it finds.
#
# CI depends on the first half: the drift gate in `.github/workflows/ci.yml` re-runs the
# generators and demands an empty `git status`. Both generators write LF, so a file checked out
# with CRLF on a maintainer's Windows machine reads as modified the moment either one runs — a
# whole-file phantom diff that `git diff` then hides, because it normalises the very difference
# `git status` is reporting.
#
# Binary files are detected, not listed: `text=auto` leaves anything with a NUL byte alone, which
# covers `public/assets/*.mp3` and `*.mp4` without naming them.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Generated. GitHub collapses these in diffs and drops them from the language statistics, which
# otherwise read this repository as one third JSON. Every one is rebuilt by `npm run
# registry:generate` or `npm run themes:generate`; see CONTRIBUTING.md.
